 ## Overview
-Hardware-Assisted Polyhedron Projection is a technique for volume rendering arbitrary unstructured grids composed of convex polyhedral cells by projecting 3D cells to 2D screen space and using hardware polygon rendering pipelines for scan conversion and compositing [S1][S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently rendering complex, non-uniform meshes by avoiding the irregular memory access patterns of raycasting, instead relying on global back-to-front sorting of cells and hardware-accelerated interpolation [S1][S4, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that may use local sorting or additive accumulation, this method requires explicit depth sorting of all cells to ensure correct alpha blending [S1][S4, unofficial].
+Hardware-Assisted Polyhedron Projection is a technique for volume rendering arbitrary unstructured grids composed of convex polyhedral cells by projecting 3D cells into 2D screen space and using hardware rasterization to compute optical properties per pixel [S1][S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently rendering complex, non-uniform volumetric data by leveraging the graphics pipeline for scan conversion and compositing, avoiding the memory bandwidth bottlenecks of raycasting [S3]. Unlike raycasting, which groups connected cells to exploit coherence, this method disperses connected cells widely to achieve static load balancing in parallel environments [S3]. It builds on [Hardware-Assisted Projection](5625-hardware-assisted-projection.md) by providing the specific geometric subdivision and data distribution strategies required for arbitrary convex polyhedra.
 
 ## How it works
-The method projects each convex polyhedral cell onto the image plane, subdivides the resulting 2D projection into regions where optical thickness varies linearly, and uses texture mapping to compute non-linear opacity per pixel [S1].
+The method projects each convex polyhedral cell onto the image plane, subdivides the projection into regions where optical thickness varies linearly, computes color and opacity for these regions, and composites them in back-to-front order using hardware blending [S1][S4, unofficial].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Input**: A set of convex polyhedral cells defined by their 3D vertices and scalar field values (extinction coefficient $\tau$) [S1][S3].
-*   **Output**: A 2D image with color and opacity values for each pixel [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **Inputs**: A set of convex polyhedral cells defining an unstructured grid; scalar values at nodes mapped to color and extinction coefficient $\tau$; camera parameters (viewpoint, projection matrix).
+- **Outputs**: A 2D image with accumulated color and opacity.
 
 ### Data Structures
 
 **Ray Segment**
-A data structure used during scan conversion and composition [S2, unofficial]:
-*   `RGB`: 3 floats (color contribution).
-*   $\alpha$: 1 float (opacity).
-*   $z_{front}$: 1 float (depth of front intersection).
-*   $z_{back}$: 1 float (depth of back intersection).
